.TH CVECTOR_RESERVE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cvector_reserve \- reserve capacity for future elements
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

bool cvector_reserve(cvec v, size_t new_capacity_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates capacity for new_capacity_count elements or more. It rounds
the capacity up to the nearest power of two (minimum 4). This helps you
to prevent many reallocations when you know the expected size in
advance.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v                    Vector to reserve capacity for
new_capacity_count   Minimum number of elements to reserve
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns true when the reservation succeeds. It also returns true when
the capacity is already large enough
Returns false in three cases. The first case is a failed allocation.
The second case is a capacity above ccol_max_elem_count. The third case
is a capacity that overflows a size_t after the multiplication by the
element size
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Does nothing when the requested capacity <= the current capacity
The real capacity can be larger, because of the rounding to a power
of 2
The function tests both limits in RETURN VALUE against the rounded-up
capacity. It does not test them against new_capacity_count itself. A
request that is inside a limit can therefore still fail after the
rounding
A vector of wide elements reaches the size_t overflow limit first.
That limit bounds the capacity at SIZE_MAX divided by the element
size. For an element wider than one byte, that limit is the lower of
the two
Does not change the element count
Asserts if v is NULL
The cvec_reserve(3) macro evaluates new_capacity_count exactly once.
This is also true on the failure path. An expression with a side
effect is therefore safe to give
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_reserve (3)
